INT. DEEP-SEA MINING SUBMERSIBLE - COCKPIT - SIX KILOMETERS DOWN

A pressurized sphere barely wider than a bathtub. Banks of dials and gauges. Three seats facing a viewport thick as a dinner plate. Beyond it, nothing. Black water, falling snow of dead plankton.

CAPTAIN ZHAO (50s, headset, steady voice) grips the yoke. ALVARO (30s, sweat-soaked polo, geologist) pores over a sonar screen. LENA (20s, rookie, braids tucked under a company cap) monitors the oxygen scrubbers.

ZHAO
Depth six-oh-two-one. Holding.

ALVARO
Sonar's picking up something big.

LENA
How big?

ALVARO
Bigger than the sub. Bigger than the seabed shelf.

ZHAO
Is it moving?

ALVARO
It's breathing.

LENA
Captain, oxygen is dipping.

ZHAO
Dipping how?

LENA
Four percent in two minutes.

ALVARO
Cap, the contact is closing.

ZHAO
Lena, shut down the non-essentials.

LENA
Shutting down.

ALVARO
It's right under us.

ZHAO
Alvaro, give me a heading.

ALVARO
Up. Any direction up.

The sub shudders. A groan rolls through the hull. Lena grips her seat. The viewport floods with a slow pale glow, climbing from below.

LENA
Oh my God.

ZHAO
Blow ballast.

ALVARO
Blowing.

LENA
It's not working.

ZHAO
Blow it again!

ALVARO
The valves are frozen.

The glow swells. Something vast turns beneath them, an eye as wide as a cathedral, and the viewport fills with its slow, gold, curious gaze.
